  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 385e88b7439744b4db7315d03e5de07f, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::RangerMenuUI
  menuRoot: {fileID: 5620181301889590315}
  mainMenuPanel: {fileID: 234922275542400599}
  responseText: {fileID: 4433165254759127956}
  customQuestionPanel: {fileID: 2740806595083139663}
  questionInput: {fileID: 6581244288692611611}
  characterScript: {fileID: 0}
  cameraScript: {fileID: 0}
  defaultMessage: Ask me about the Founding Era of Yellowstone, from 1872 to 1886.
  eraName: Founding Era (1872 - 1886)
  historyResponse: "Welcome to Yellowstone\u2019s Founding Era. In 1872, Congress
    created Yellowstone National Park, and President Ulysses S. Grant signed it into
    law after expeditions confirmed the region\u2019s extraordinary features. Early
    management was difficult. Nathaniel Langford tried to oversee the park for years
    without funding, staff, buildings, or clear laws. Later, Philetus Norris became
    superintendent and made major improvements, including building headquarters at
    Mammoth Hot Springs and starting a primitive road system. Even so, the park still
    struggled with poachers, squatters, woodcutters, and vandals. This was also a
    time when many Native American tribes with long histories in the region were
    being displaced from lands they had used for generations."
  wildlifeResponse: "Wildlife in Yellowstone\u2019s Founding Era faced heavy pressure.
    Poaching was a major problem, so animal populations were lower than they should
    have been. Bison were especially threatened and were close to disappearing from
    the park. Visitors in this period could still encounter elk, gray wolves, bears,
    and mountain lions, but protection was weak and enforcement was limited. Because
    the park had few staff and almost no resources at first, wildlife was far more
    vulnerable than it is today."
  buildingsResponse: Buildings and infrastructure in the Founding Era were very limited
    and practical. The park had primitive wagon roads rather than the developed road
    system visitors know today. One of the most important structures was the headquarters
    at Mammoth Hot Springs, built under Superintendent Philetus Norris. You would
    also have seen Native American teepees in the region, reflecting the presence
    of tribes who had long-standing ties to this land. Overall, Yellowstone had far
    fewer buildings, and most construction was basic, scattered, and focused on simple
    access and administration.
  rangerApiUrl: http://localhost:3000/ask-ranger
